\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The three-dimensional analogue. (a) The all-contact corner: the unit ball about the centre (blue), its twelve contact points (clay), and the twelve unit balls that touch it, at distance $2$ in the contact directions of the face-centred cubic lattice. (b) The Voronoi cell of the centre, the rhombic dodecahedron, with one facet at distance $1$ for each contact and volume $4\sqrt  2=5.6569$. (c) Monotonicity in the radii: one neighbour moves from distance $2$ out to $2.6$, its half-space recedes, and the cell grows from $5.6569$ to $5.9666$; the ochre solid is the volume gained. (d) A deviation: one contact direction is tilted by $25^\circ $ at distance $2$; the cell gains the teal volume and loses the clay volume, and rises from $5.6569$ to $5.7470$. The volumes are computed from the half-space intersections.}}{7}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:corner}{{2}{7}{The three-dimensional analogue. (a) The all-contact corner: the unit ball about the centre (blue), its twelve contact points (clay), and the twelve unit balls that touch it, at distance $2$ in the contact directions of the face-centred cubic lattice. (b) The Voronoi cell of the centre, the rhombic dodecahedron, with one facet at distance $1$ for each contact and volume $4\sqrt 2=5.6569$. (c) Monotonicity in the radii: one neighbour moves from distance $2$ out to $2.6$, its half-space recedes, and the cell grows from $5.6569$ to $5.9666$; the ochre solid is the volume gained. (d) A deviation: one contact direction is tilted by $25^\circ $ at distance $2$; the cell gains the teal volume and loses the clay volume, and rises from $5.6569$ to $5.7470$. The volumes are computed from the half-space intersections}{figure.2}{}}
