\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Steps\nonbreakingspace 3 and\nonbreakingspace 5 of the verification of the certificate. (a) The size of step\nonbreakingspace 3. The integral over $\mathrm  O(4)$ is taken one monomial triple at a time; the parity rule on the exponent matrix discards all but about a thirtieth of the triples, invariance under permuting rows and columns collapses the rest onto at most $2\,505\,430$ canonical forms in the largest of the $490$ entries, and the answer never has more than $\binom  {20}{6}=38\,760$ coefficients. (b) Step\nonbreakingspace 5 for the four-point identity. The curve is the number of monomials of the zonal side as the signatures are added, through the three ways of splitting four points into two pairs; it ends at $53\,572$, the support of the sum-of-squares side as well. The two sides have the same support and opposite coefficients, so their sum is zero at each of the $53\,572$ monomials.}}{42}{figure.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:verify}{{10}{42}{Steps~3 and~5 of the verification of the certificate. (a) The size of step~3. The integral over $\mathrm O(4)$ is taken one monomial triple at a time; the parity rule on the exponent matrix discards all but about a thirtieth of the triples, invariance under permuting rows and columns collapses the rest onto at most $2\,505\,430$ canonical forms in the largest of the $490$ entries, and the answer never has more than $\binom {20}{6}=38\,760$ coefficients. (b) Step~5 for the four-point identity. The curve is the number of monomials of the zonal side as the signatures are added, through the three ways of splitting four points into two pairs; it ends at $53\,572$, the support of the sum-of-squares side as well. The two sides have the same support and opposite coefficients, so their sum is zero at each of the $53\,572$ monomials}{figure.10}{}}
\newlabel{fig:verify@cref}{{[figure][10][]10}{[1][41][]42}}
