\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces (a) The neighbour sum in dimension three, where it fits in one picture: a contact direction of the face-centred cubic lattice (teal), the four contact directions at $60^\circ $ from it (clay), and their sum (ochre), which is twice the direction itself. (b) The same identity in dimension four, drawn where it lives. The eight roots at inner product $1$ from a root $\alpha $ fill the hyperplane $\langle \alpha ,x\rangle =1$, a copy of $\mathbb  {R}^3$, as the vertices of a cube centred at $\alpha /2$ (dark); eight times that centre is $4\alpha $. (c) The singular values of the rigidity operator $\Lambda $ on the $72$-dimensional tangent space, with multiplicities (\cref  {lem:rigidity-spectrum}). The kernel is the six infinitesimal rotations, and the smallest nonzero value is one of the two constants that turn the first-order argument into a radius; the other is the weight $\tfrac  {11}{16}$ that every pair carries in the image of $\Lambda $. (d) The two sides of the inequality that closes \cref  {thm:local-uniqueness}. Feasibility forces $\|\varepsilon \|^2\le \tfrac  {397}{4}\|\varepsilon \|^4$, so the displacement is either zero or at least $2/\sqrt  {397}$: the shaded band holds no contact configuration of $24$ directions.}}{57}{figure.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rig}{{13}{57}{(a) The neighbour sum in dimension three, where it fits in one picture: a contact direction of the face-centred cubic lattice (teal), the four contact directions at $60^\circ $ from it (clay), and their sum (ochre), which is twice the direction itself. (b) The same identity in dimension four, drawn where it lives. The eight roots at inner product $1$ from a root $\alpha $ fill the hyperplane $\langle \alpha ,x\rangle =1$, a copy of $\mathbb {R}^3$, as the vertices of a cube centred at $\alpha /2$ (dark); eight times that centre is $4\alpha $. (c) The singular values of the rigidity operator $\Lambda $ on the $72$-dimensional tangent space, with multiplicities (\cref {lem:rigidity-spectrum}). The kernel is the six infinitesimal rotations, and the smallest nonzero value is one of the two constants that turn the first-order argument into a radius; the other is the weight $\tfrac {11}{16}$ that every pair carries in the image of $\Lambda $. (d) The two sides of the inequality that closes \cref {thm:local-uniqueness}. Feasibility forces $\|\varepsilon \|^2\le \tfrac {397}{4}\|\varepsilon \|^4$, so the displacement is either zero or at least $2/\sqrt {397}$: the shaded band holds no contact configuration of $24$ directions}{figure.13}{}}
\newlabel{fig:rig@cref}{{[figure][13][]13}{[1][56][]57}}
