\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {23}{\ignorespaces The two halves of \cref  {thm:m24} at positive slack. (a) The three-point bound on the number of points of $S^3$ with inner products at most $\tfrac  12+s$, from sampled programmes of degree $8$ and $10$ (each sampled value is slightly optimistic), with the three certified values: degree $8$ at $s=0.005$ and $0.0065$, degree $10$ at $s=0.008$, the one \cref  {thm:kissing-stable} uses. It crosses $25$ near $s=0.009$; a $25$-point code exists from $s=0.03743$ on. (b) How far the proof of \cref  {thm:m24-robust} could be pushed if the triple and quadruple terms cost nothing ($B_3=B_4=0$): the largest slack $\kappa $ it reaches, against the tolerance $\delta $ that a rounding lemma would allow (\cref  {rem:robust-ceiling}). The curve grows like $\delta ^2$ and stops below $10^{-8}$; the proof as it stands gives $2\cdot 10^{-26}$.}}{80}{figure.23}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:positive-slack}{{23}{80}{The two halves of \cref {thm:m24} at positive slack. (a) The three-point bound on the number of points of $S^3$ with inner products at most $\tfrac 12+s$, from sampled programmes of degree $8$ and $10$ (each sampled value is slightly optimistic), with the three certified values: degree $8$ at $s=0.005$ and $0.0065$, degree $10$ at $s=0.008$, the one \cref {thm:kissing-stable} uses. It crosses $25$ near $s=0.009$; a $25$-point code exists from $s=0.03743$ on. (b) How far the proof of \cref {thm:m24-robust} could be pushed if the triple and quadruple terms cost nothing ($B_3=B_4=0$): the largest slack $\kappa $ it reaches, against the tolerance $\delta $ that a rounding lemma would allow (\cref {rem:robust-ceiling}). The curve grows like $\delta ^2$ and stops below $10^{-8}$; the proof as it stands gives $2\cdot 10^{-26}$}{figure.23}{}}
