\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25}{\ignorespaces The cap argument of \cref  {sec:cap-theorem}. (a) The plane $x_2=x_3=0$ in the frame of four orthogonal roots, the deviating root being $e_0$, to scale. The cross-polytope $B$ (dashed) meets it in a square, the polytope $Q$ of the twenty-three undeviated roots in the pentagon outlined in blue, and the $24$-cell $\mathcal  V$ in the inner square; the dotted circle is the unit sphere. The half-space $x_0\ge 1$ cuts the same piece off $Q$ and off $B$ (orange), of volume $\frac  13$ in $\mathbb  {R}^4$, so that $\vol  Q=8+\frac  13$. A deviated contact replaces it by a half-space $\langle x,w\rangle \ge 1$ tangent to the unit sphere (green). (b) The volume that $\langle x,u\rangle \ge 1$ cuts off $B$, from \cref  {lem:cap-formula}, along two arcs of unit normals $u$ leaving the vertex direction $e_0$: towards the midpoint of an edge, $u=(\cos t,\sin t,0,0)$, and towards the centre of a facet, $u=(\cos t,s,s,s)$ with $s=\sin t/\sqrt  3$. Both stay below $\frac  13$ for $t>0$, as \cref  {thm:cap-inequality} asserts for every $u$. In the plane of panel (a) alone the comparison would go the other way, which is why the theorem needs the four-dimensional cap formula.}}{87}{figure.25}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cap-scheme}{{25}{87}{The cap argument of \cref {sec:cap-theorem}. (a) The plane $x_2=x_3=0$ in the frame of four orthogonal roots, the deviating root being $e_0$, to scale. The cross-polytope $B$ (dashed) meets it in a square, the polytope $Q$ of the twenty-three undeviated roots in the pentagon outlined in blue, and the $24$-cell $\mathcal V$ in the inner square; the dotted circle is the unit sphere. The half-space $x_0\ge 1$ cuts the same piece off $Q$ and off $B$ (orange), of volume $\frac 13$ in $\mathbb {R}^4$, so that $\vol Q=8+\frac 13$. A deviated contact replaces it by a half-space $\langle x,w\rangle \ge 1$ tangent to the unit sphere (green). (b) The volume that $\langle x,u\rangle \ge 1$ cuts off $B$, from \cref {lem:cap-formula}, along two arcs of unit normals $u$ leaving the vertex direction $e_0$: towards the midpoint of an edge, $u=(\cos t,\sin t,0,0)$, and towards the centre of a facet, $u=(\cos t,s,s,s)$ with $s=\sin t/\sqrt 3$. Both stay below $\frac 13$ for $t>0$, as \cref {thm:cap-inequality} asserts for every $u$. In the plane of panel (a) alone the comparison would go the other way, which is why the theorem needs the four-dimensional cap formula}{figure.25}{}}
\newlabel{fig:cap-scheme@cref}{{[figure][25][]25}{[1][86][]87}}
