\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28}{\ignorespaces Two elementary facts behind \cref  {sec:closure}, to scale. (a) \Cref  {lem:no-triple}: four points pairwise at least $2$ apart do not fit in a ball of radius less than $\sqrt  {3/2}$, and the regular tetrahedron of edge $2$ in the ball of radius $\sqrt  {3/2}$ is the extreme case; so no point $x$ of the open ball $B(\sqrt  {3/2})$ about the centre $0$ is closer to three other centres than to $0$. (b) \Cref  {lem:holes} in a plane section: the balls of the contacts (green) leave a hole of angular radius $\rho $, here $50^\circ $, and a further centre $z$ can enter it only at a distance $|z|\ge 4\cos \rho $ from $c$, where it meets the circles of radius $2$ (dashed) about the two contacts at the rim.}}{93}{figure.28}\protected@file@percent }
