\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29}{\ignorespaces (a) \Cref  {cor:root-subsets}: a vertex $v$ of the $24$-cell and its three orthogonal pairs of normalised roots, in the linear projection of $\mathbb  {R}^4$ that keeps the vertex direction vertical and sends the other three coordinate directions to horizontal unit vectors at $120^\circ $. The six roots at $v$ lie on a hexagon, the points $2u$ on a larger one, and $v$ is the midpoint of the chord from $2u$ to $2u'$ of each pair, so it lies in the inversion hull of any contact set that keeps one pair. One root is missing (hollow), its chord is lost (dashed), and $v$ keeps the other two. (b) \Cref  {prop:inversion-hull} in three dimensions: the Voronoi cell of the twelve contacts of the face-centred cubic packing, a rhombic dodecahedron (blue), lies in the convex hull of the points $2u$ (orange) and touches its boundary only at its six four-valent vertices (black), as the $24$-cell touches the hull of the points $2u$ at its $24$ vertices; every other centre leaves that hull whole. (c) \Cref  {thm:near-contact} to first order: the exact volume excess of the root system pushed out uniformly, $8(1+\delta /2)^4-8$ with $\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ \delta _i=24\delta $, and of the deletion with one centre at $2+\delta $ on the deleted axis, $\frac  13-\frac  13(1-\frac  \delta 2)^4$, against the total excess distance; both are tangent at the root system to $\frac  23\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ \delta _i$.}}{95}{figure.29}\protected@file@percent }
