\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {32}{\ignorespaces Why the pair terms stop at twenty-three centres, to scale. (a) The section of the $24$-cell $Q$ by the plane $x_3=x_4=0$: the square $|x_1|+|x_2|\le \sqrt  2$, whose sides touch the unit ball and whose corners are vertices of $Q$ at radius $\sqrt  2$. The ball $B(\sqrt  {3/2})$, inside which \cref  {lem:no-triple} makes the cell an exact function of the pairs, cuts the corners off (orange). The four centres of the plane, $\sqrt  2(\pm e_1\pm e_2)$ at distance $2$, with their unit balls, and their inversion hull (dashed), which meets $Q$ only at its vertices. (b) One corner, magnified: the vertex $v=\sqrt  2\,e_1$, the two sides through it and the side $x_1=\sqrt  2$ of the hull; the corner is $\sqrt  2-\sqrt  {3/2}=0.189$ deep. In $\mathbb  {R}^4$ the $24$ corners hold $8-7.906940=0.093060$ of the volume of $Q$.}}{99}{figure.32}\protected@file@percent }
