\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {37}{\ignorespaces The curve of \cref  {prop:hexagon-loop}, drawn at $\theta =17^\circ $. (a) The plane $P$: the six roots of the hexagon turned by $\theta $ from the reference direction (dotted), the hexagon $\mathrm  {Hex}_\theta $ of inradius $1$ that they cut out, the directions in $P$ of the other eighteen roots, at radius $\sigma $ (filled: the triangle $T_+$ at $30^\circ ,150^\circ ,270^\circ $; open: $T_-$), and the sector between the rays at $30^\circ $ and $90^\circ $, split at the vertex $v$ into the two triangles (blue and green) over which $J_1$ and $J_2$ are computed. (b) The slice over a point of $\mathrm  {Hex}_\theta $: the intersection of two opposite triangles of inradii $h_+$ and $h_-$ in $P'$, a hexagon while $h_+\le 2h_-$ and $h_-\le 2h_+$. (c) The cell as the union of its slices over $\mathrm  {Hex}_\theta $, with the exact slices over three points; the two-dimensional slices are drawn in one plane transverse to $P$. (d) Along the curve: the tilt $\sin \psi $ of the eighteen roots and the largest inner product, which exceeds $\frac  12$ for $0<\theta <\pi /3$ and reaches $\frac  47$ at $\theta =\pi /6$.}}{111}{figure.37}\protected@file@percent }
