\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {45}{\ignorespaces (a) The four breakpoint curves of the first arc, drawn to scale from the closed forms of \cref  {thm:arc1-breakpoints-new}: the universal curve $\varphi =\pi /3$ and the curves $\mathsf  A$, $\mathsf  W$, $\mathsf  Y$. They meet in exactly three points, at the abscissae $s_1$, $s_2$, $s_3$ listed at the right, and the eight combinatorial regions of \cref  {sec:region-count-correction} are the cells of the resulting arrangement. The curves $\mathsf  A$ and $\mathsf  W$ meet only at $s=\pi /4$, where the reflection symmetry of the arc identifies them. (b) The four breakpoint curves of the second arc, drawn to scale from the closed forms of \cref  {thm:arc2-breakpoints-new}. Comparing with \cref  {fig:arcs}(a) shows what \cref  {prop:arc2-is-arc1} later proves: the three equations here are exactly those of $\mathsf  A$, $\mathsf  W$ and $\mathsf  Y$ on the first arc, and the arrangement, crossings included, is the same one, even though the curves come from different vertices of $\mathcal  {V}$. The band immediately below region top splits into three sub-parts at the crossings $s_1,s_2,s_3$.}}{147}{figure.45}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:arcs}{{45}{147}{(a) The four breakpoint curves of the first arc, drawn to scale from the closed forms of \cref {thm:arc1-breakpoints-new}: the universal curve $\tilt =\pi /3$ and the curves $\mathsf A$, $\mathsf W$, $\mathsf Y$. They meet in exactly three points, at the abscissae $\arcp _1$, $\arcp _2$, $\arcp _3$ listed at the right, and the eight combinatorial regions of \cref {sec:region-count-correction} are the cells of the resulting arrangement. The curves $\mathsf A$ and $\mathsf W$ meet only at $\arcp =\pi /4$, where the reflection symmetry of the arc identifies them. (b) The four breakpoint curves of the second arc, drawn to scale from the closed forms of \cref {thm:arc2-breakpoints-new}. Comparing with \cref {fig:arcs}(a) shows what \cref {prop:arc2-is-arc1} later proves: the three equations here are exactly those of $\mathsf A$, $\mathsf W$ and $\mathsf Y$ on the first arc, and the arrangement, crossings included, is the same one, even though the curves come from different vertices of $\VCell $. The band immediately below region top splits into three sub-parts at the crossings $\arcp _1,\arcp _2,\arcp _3$}{figure.45}{}}
\newlabel{fig:arcs@cref}{{[figure][45][2147483647]45}{[1][146][]147}}
