\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {48}{\ignorespaces (a) The volume defect along the three swap paths of this subsection, over the whole range $(0,\theta _*)$, $\theta _*=\pi /3$. The vertical scale is logarithmic because the three sit nearly three orders of magnitude apart: the two-direction and three-direction paths peak at $0.2317$ and $0.1692$ at the midpoint, the six-direction path at $6.4\times 10^{-4}$. All three are positive throughout and vanish at both ends, as \cref  {thm:swap-m2-new,thm:swap-m3-new,thm:swap-m6-new} assert exactly. The dashed line is the single combinatorial transition, at $\theta =\pi /6$, visible as the corner in the $m=2$ curve; the $m=6$ path has none, and its curve is smooth there. The values plotted are enumerated cell volumes, computed by a route independent of the closed forms, and for $m=6$ they agree with the closed form of \cref  {sec:swap-m6} to $9\times 10^{-9}$. (b) The swap path against the mixed path, the two differing only in the sign $c_1$. The more adversarial of the two is the lower one, and which that is changes at $\theta =0.6492$: below the crossover the mixed path is lower, at $\theta =0.05$ by $0.000834$ against $0.002474$, and above it the swap path is, through $\theta _*$ and beyond. Second-order theory cannot tell the two apart, since the Hessian entry that distinguishes them enters only through its magnitude. The zero of the swap curve at $\theta _*$ is the swap point itself, where the configuration is again a root system.}}{164}{figure.48}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:swap}{{48}{164}{(a) The volume defect along the three swap paths of this subsection, over the whole range $(0,\theta _*)$, $\theta _*=\pi /3$. The vertical scale is logarithmic because the three sit nearly three orders of magnitude apart: the two-direction and three-direction paths peak at $0.2317$ and $0.1692$ at the midpoint, the six-direction path at $6.4\times 10^{-4}$. All three are positive throughout and vanish at both ends, as \cref {thm:swap-m2-new,thm:swap-m3-new,thm:swap-m6-new} assert exactly. The dashed line is the single combinatorial transition, at $\theta =\pi /6$, visible as the corner in the $m=2$ curve; the $m=6$ path has none, and its curve is smooth there. The values plotted are enumerated cell volumes, computed by a route independent of the closed forms, and for $m=6$ they agree with the closed form of \cref {sec:swap-m6} to $9\times 10^{-9}$. (b) The swap path against the mixed path, the two differing only in the sign $c_1$. The more adversarial of the two is the lower one, and which that is changes at $\theta =0.6492$: below the crossover the mixed path is lower, at $\theta =0.05$ by $0.000834$ against $0.002474$, and above it the swap path is, through $\theta _*$ and beyond. Second-order theory cannot tell the two apart, since the Hessian entry that distinguishes them enters only through its magnitude. The zero of the swap curve at $\theta _*$ is the swap point itself, where the configuration is again a root system}{figure.48}{}}
\newlabel{fig:swap@cref}{{[figure][48][2147483647]48}{[1][163][]164}}
